WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.320
DC's cinematic history spans over half a&nbsp;
century - and in that time they've released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.440
some of the most memorable comic book movies&nbsp;
ever made. From Batman's beginnings to Zack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:17.360
Snyder's Justice League, these are the most&nbsp;
iconic scenes from every live action DC movie.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.600
1966's Batman contains a scene that perfectly&nbsp;
encapsulates the campy tone of '60s-era Batman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.480
Upon discovering a smoking bomb in the United&nbsp;
Underworld's HQ, Batman dashes out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.760
building to dispose of it. Unfortunately, he&nbsp;
runs into... well, basically everything - from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.520
mother with a baby to a gaggle of nuns to a full&nbsp;
brass band. Cue Batman's now-legendary lament:

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.640
"Some days you just can't get rid of a bomb."

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.320
Luckily, the ever-resourceful Caped Crusader&nbsp;
eventually manages to dispose of the bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.920
while ducking beside some lead pipes&nbsp;
to shield himself from the blast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:00.800
Utterly ridiculous and totally hilarious, this&nbsp;
scene is Adam West's Batman at its absolute best.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.800
Considered to be a classic film in its own&nbsp;
right, 1978's Superman was one of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.760
DC films to actually take its hero seriously -&nbsp;
and part of that means following a young Clark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.640
Kent's life in Smallville. As such, by the end&nbsp;
of the movie's first act, most audiences will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.360
eager to see Superman make his proper debut.&nbsp;
Luckily, they won't be disappointed, either.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.640
Although the Man of Steel first&nbsp;
appears in the Fortress of Solitude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.760
he truly reveals himself to the world after a&nbsp;
disastrous helicopter takeoff threatens the life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.280
of Lois Lane. Seeing he's needed, Clark Kent&nbsp;
races across the street, rips open his shirt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.240
and reveals the famous "S" emblem on his&nbsp;
chest. After spinning through a revolving door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.120
Superman takes to the sky and catches Lois&nbsp;
mid-fall. Accompanied by John Williams'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:51.182
classic Superman score, this iconic moment set&nbsp;
the standard for all future Superman movies.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.920
Superman II sees Clark Kent sacrifice&nbsp;
his powers to be with Lois Lane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.640
Unfortunately, his decision coincides with the&nbsp;
reappearance of a trio of Kryptonian criminals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.760
led by the nefarious General Zod. Realizing&nbsp;
that only Superman can stop this threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.520
Clark manages to re-energize his&nbsp;
powers at the Fortress of Solitude.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.840
Back in Metropolis, Zod and his cronies&nbsp;
invade the Daily Planet and threaten to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:20.000
everyone in the building - unless the "son of&nbsp;
Jor-El" appears. Just as all hope seems lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.221
the wind blows, the music swells, and a&nbsp;
familiar caped figure soars into the room.

00:02:25.221 --> 00:02:29.360
"General, would you care to step outside?"

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.600
Superman is famous for his dramatic&nbsp;
entrances and last-second arrivals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.653
and this moment is the best of the very best.

00:02:37.486 --> 00:02:41.360
The first Swamp Thing movie was released&nbsp;
when comic book films were still trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.280
to strike a balance between serious&nbsp;
storytelling and campy comedy. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.080
the main character comes across as kind of&nbsp;
a B-movie monster, albeit a more heroic one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.800
It only seems natural, then, that the scene in&nbsp;
which Swamp Thing fights his archenemy, Arcane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.120
is legendarily cheesy. Both characters&nbsp;
are obviously wearing rubber suits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.520
and the bestial Arcane for some reason&nbsp;
decides to use a sword against his opponent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:09.874
This is comic book cinema at its absolute goofiest&nbsp;
— not that there's anything wrong with that.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.480
Despite its reputation as one of the weaker&nbsp;
entries in the Superman movie franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.240
Superman III contains one of the most&nbsp;
iconic scenes in Superman movie history:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.320
The eerie sequence in which Clark Kent&nbsp;
battles Superman. After Gus Gorman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.640
creates a synthetic form of Kryptonite, he&nbsp;
exposes Superman to the toxic substance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.480
which eventually splits the hero into two&nbsp;
beings: A good Clark Kent and an evil Superman.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.600
The two then come to blows in a junkyard, where&nbsp;
Superman tries to destroy his nerdy alter ego,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.720
once and for all. Clark refuses to succumb,&nbsp;
however, and finally strangles his corrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.200
counterpart, causing the evil Superman to&nbsp;
vanish into thin air. In the aftermath,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:53.520
Clark rips open his shirt, showing off his bright&nbsp;
"S" shield, and soars away as Superman reborn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.834
It's a magnificent sequence in - let's&nbsp;
face it - a less-than-magnificent movie.

00:03:59.834 --> 00:04:04.400
Though it ultimately failed to expand the&nbsp;
Superman movie franchise, Supergirl still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:07.920
offers a memorable performance&nbsp;
by Helen Slater as Kara Zor-El.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.000
And arguably her finest moment takes&nbsp;
place when she first arrives on Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.880
where she discovers both the wonders of&nbsp;
the natural world and her new superpowers.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.680
Kara picks up a rock and accidentally crushes&nbsp;
it in her hand. She plucks a flower, makes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:26.400
bloom with her heat vision, and is shocked and&nbsp;
delighted when she finds herself floating. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.520
she grows more comfortable somersaulting through&nbsp;
the air, Supergirl takes her first proper flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.520
soaring through the clouds and finally&nbsp;
settling down to watch the sunset.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.480
This is a genuinely beautiful scene, unlike&nbsp;
any you'll find in the other Superman movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.259
It's just a shame the rest of&nbsp;
the movie doesn't measure up.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.360
Although it's generally regarded&nbsp;
as the worst Superman movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.400
one sequence in Superman IV stands out: The battle&nbsp;
between Superman and his solar-powered clone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.320
Nuclear Man. Created by Lex Luthor to&nbsp;
destroy the Man of Steel, Nuclear Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.280
decides to first "have fun" by flying around&nbsp;
the world and causing a number of disasters.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:04.640
Naturally, Superman follows&nbsp;
Nuclear Man and manages to set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.880
everything right - although budget constraints&nbsp;
and some weak storytelling leads him to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.200
some strange new powers in the process.&nbsp;
Still, despite the poor special effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:17.210
it's actually a lot of fun seeing Nuclear&nbsp;
Man wreak havoc on famous world landmarks.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:24.160
By 1989, Swamp Thing had undergone a revival in&nbsp;
DC comics, thanks to superstar writer Alan Moore's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.720
reconfiguration of the character. The Return&nbsp;
of Swamp Thing largely ignored these changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.240
however, opting instead to lean into&nbsp;
the comic B-movie side of things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.480
Dick Durock reprises his role as Swamp Thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.560
but displays a more casual attitude as he&nbsp;
takes the fight to several new monsters.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.640
One particularly funny scene unfolds when&nbsp;
a baseball bat-wielding Swamp Thing saves a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:46.800
couple kids from some bad guys. When the kids&nbsp;
try to take a photo of him for the tabloids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.480
Swamp Thing not only obliges, he has one&nbsp;
of the kids pose with him in the shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.720
If only Bigfoot was this accommodating.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:59.440
Tim Burton's 1989 film Batman set a new tone&nbsp;
for live-action depictions of the Dark Knight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.640
No longer the campy crimefighter of the 1960s&nbsp;
TV show, Batman could now be portrayed as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:10.560
the brooding, serious hero he was in the comic&nbsp;
books. And nowhere is this made more clear than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.720
with his first appearance, in which he steps&nbsp;
out of the shadows to take down two muggers.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:16.240
"What are you?"

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:18.800
"I'm Batman."

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:25.600
With these two simple words, Keaton set a whole&nbsp;
new precedent for the character of Batman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:30.880
Unsurprisingly, this iconic line has been&nbsp;
repeated in practically every Batman film and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:36.821
TV series since, as it so effectively communicates&nbsp;
everything you need to know about the Dark Knight.

00:06:36.821 --> 00:06:39.440
"I am Batman!"

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:40.560
"I'm Batman."

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.000
"I'm Batman."

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.280
"Hey, I'm Batman."

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.400
"I'm Batman."

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:45.727
"I'm Batman."

00:06:45.727 --> 00:06:47.428
"I'm Batman."

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:55.878
And we all know&nbsp;exactly what that means - because Keaton made&nbsp;
it perfectly clear the first time around.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.960
One thing that's certain about Tim Burton's&nbsp;
Batman movies is that the villains almost always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.680
overshadow the hero. Take Catwoman, for example.&nbsp;
Michelle Pfeiffer's Selina Kyle goes through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.000
dramatic character arc in the film, starting&nbsp;
out as a humble secretary to her corrupt boss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.680
Max Shreck. After he pushes her out&nbsp;
of a window, Selina is revived by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.160
a colony of cats, and transforms into&nbsp;
a more aggressive version of herself.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:23.360
Her first big action scene establishes&nbsp;
exactly who this Catwoman is. When she sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.600
a mugger assault a young woman, Catwoman&nbsp;
brutally kicks the thug into submission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.440
clawing his face into a bloody pulp&nbsp;
for good measure. And then she does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.440
something even more surprising: she&nbsp;
assaults the would-be victim, too.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:41.727
"You make it so easy, don't you? Always&nbsp;
waiting for some Batman to save you."

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:43.840
That's Selina Kyle, alright.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:50.640
Say what you will about the Joel Schumacher Batman&nbsp;
films, but they at least have a distinctive style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.480
all their own. Batman Forever sets that&nbsp;
tone right away in its opening scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:58.000
in which Bruce Wayne dons his&nbsp;
Batsuit and chooses his weapons.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:02.640
This scene is so instantly memorable that&nbsp;
it's even parodied within the same movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:06.480
when Bruce dons a new prototype suit&nbsp;
and gives the audience a close-up of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:11.680
his now-infamous "Bat butt." The sequence is&nbsp;
also repeated in the sequel, Batman &amp; Robin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:15.840
and is now considered one of the Dark Knight's&nbsp;
most infamous and embarrassing moments.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:21.040
Batman &amp; Robin is a movie that seems&nbsp;
dead-set on one-upping itself with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.440
increasingly bizarre and ridiculous&nbsp;
moments. One scene, however, elevates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:30.400
the silliness to almost unbelievable levels.&nbsp;
While appearing at a charity benefit auction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:35.440
Batman and Robin are exposed to pheromone-laden&nbsp;
love dust, courtesy of Poison Ivy. They then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:40.240
proceed to bid against each other for the right&nbsp;
to date the villainess. And then… this happens.

00:08:40.602 --> 00:08:41.687
"Seven million…

00:08:44.963 --> 00:08:46.528
 never leave the cave without it."

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.600
Although the Bat-credit card is one of the most&nbsp;
heavily-ridiculed gadgets in the Caped Crusader's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:56.560
long history, it is at least a fun call-back to&nbsp;
the endearing absurdity of the '60s version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:01.920
Batman. And, if nothing else, it also emphasizes&nbsp;
Bruce Wayne's one true superpower: his money.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:07.440
In the comics, Steel is John Henry Irons, a&nbsp;
weapons designer who builds an armored suit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.320
to honor Superman after the Man of Steel dies&nbsp;
fighting Doomsday. The movie version, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:17.280
simply has John Henry and his partner Susan Sparks&nbsp;
build the suit to take down a weapons dealer.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:22.400
In the film's climax, Burke's thugs&nbsp;
capture Sparks. But, as she quickly shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:27.680
she's installed a few surprises in her chair.&nbsp;
While the film was rightly panned on its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.200
this scene is, at least, surprisingly&nbsp;
memorable for its sheer ridiculousness alone.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:40.160
The 2004 Catwoman movie has been widely&nbsp;
criticized for its nonsensical plot, bad acting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:45.760
and poor special effects. Given that the film&nbsp;
was nominated for seven Golden Raspberry Awards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:50.640
it won't surprise you to learn that the movie's&nbsp;
most iconic scene isn't exactly a great one.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.280
The scene in question is the infamous basketball&nbsp;
scrimmage between Patience and her love interest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:59.840
Detective Tom Lone. After being challenged&nbsp;
to a one-on-one basketball game by some kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:03.760
Patience uses her cat-like coordination&nbsp;
and agility to flirt with Lone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:08.000
eventually dunking the ball from the&nbsp;
free-throw line. It's a silly, unnecessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:13.200
and horribly edited scene that is thoroughly&nbsp;
ridiculed to this day… much like the movie itself.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:18.800
Keanu Reeves may have seemed like a&nbsp;
strange choice to play DC's chain-smoking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.520
magic-wielding anti-hero Constantine at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:24.880
but the film has since gone on to&nbsp;
gain something of a cult following.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:29.200
One of the movie's best scenes comes when&nbsp;
Constantine attempts to find a detective's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:33.840
twin sister by going to Hell and looking for&nbsp;
her there. In the process, he braves an army&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:38.320
of demons to snatch her hospital wristband,&nbsp;
hoping to at least return it to her sister.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:43.360
That image of Constantine reaching upward&nbsp;
for the wristband as devils attempt to drag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:48.240
him down to Hell perfectly symbolizes the&nbsp;
character's struggles with his own demons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.680
It's not exactly subtle, but this is a&nbsp;
great sequence in a surprisingly good movie.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:00.560
Batman Begins was arguably the first Batman&nbsp;
film that was truly about Batman. Offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:04.720
an in-depth look at the childhood trauma that&nbsp;
shapes Bruce Wayne, the movie spends much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:10.080
its runtime showing Bruce training and growing&nbsp;
before he finally reveals himself as Batman.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:15.360
And it's one of these moments that stands out as&nbsp;
its most iconic scene. At the start of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:20.000
a young Bruce Wayne develops a phobia of bats&nbsp;
after falling into a cave near Wayne Manor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.480
Later, having lost his parents and mastered&nbsp;
his fears under the guidance of Ra's al Ghul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:29.440
Bruce returns to the cave, and is once&nbsp;
again attacked by bats. This time, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:34.640
Bruce remains calm and lets the bats circle&nbsp;
him, immersing himself in his worst fear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:40.400
He might not be wearing the suit yet, but in&nbsp;
that moment, Bruce Wayne truly becomes Batman.

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:45.600
Bryan Singer's Superman Returns was intended&nbsp;
to be a continuation of Christopher Reeve's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:49.920
Superman films, but was criticized for&nbsp;
its somber depiction of the Man of Steel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:53.680
and the unnecessary introduction&nbsp;
of Superman's son, Jason.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:57.920
Nevertheless, Superman Returns is filled&nbsp;
with plenty of visually stunning sequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:02.880
in which Superman shows off his god-like&nbsp;
abilities. One particularly memorable scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:08.320
occurs shortly after Superman's return, when he&nbsp;
floats above the Earth and uses his super-hearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:13.440
to listen to all the voices on the planet.&nbsp;
As soon as he hears a single cry for help,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:18.720
he leaps into action and plummets back down&nbsp;
to Earth. Whatever his flaws may be, Superman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:24.490
is supposed to be Earth's greatest protector&nbsp;
- and this scene demonstrates that in full.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:32.240
Heath Ledger's magnetic performance as the Joker&nbsp;
in The Dark Knight earned him a well-deserved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:37.760
posthumous Oscar. And while virtually all of&nbsp;
Ledger's scenes could be considered iconic in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:43.680
their own right, the interrogation scene between&nbsp;
Batman and the Joker is probably the best of all.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.880
In this scene, the Joker takes&nbsp;
the opportunity offered by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:51.840
face-to-face with Batman to deliver some of&nbsp;
the best lines in comic book movie history.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:57.734
"See, I'm not a monster. I'm&nbsp;
just ahead of the curve."

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:04.640
This is a truly gripping scene, made even more&nbsp;
disturbing by the fact that the Joker makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:09.680
no effort to defend himself when things get&nbsp;
violent - yet still manages to get everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:15.440
he wants. As far as depictions of the Joker go,&nbsp;
it really doesn't get much better than this.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.800
While there are a few slight differences between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:22.400
Zack Snyder's Watchmen adaptation&nbsp;
and the comic on which it's based,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:27.680
one iconic scene is especially faithful to the&nbsp;
original work: The birth of Doctor Manhattan.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:30.640
Being the only character in the&nbsp;
story with actual superpowers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:35.920
Doctor Manhattan's creation is suitably dramatic.&nbsp;
After being accidentally locked in a test chamber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:41.920
scientist Jon Osterman is literally torn apart by&nbsp;
an experimental generator and left as a powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:46.880
disembodied consciousness. Over the next&nbsp;
few months, Jon learns to rebuild himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:50.480
finally re-appearing as an&nbsp;
omnipotent blue superman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:56.160
Narrated to perfection by Billy Crudup, the&nbsp;
entire sequence is both haunting and riveting.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:03.200
Years before he made history as Thanos in&nbsp;
the MCU, Josh Brolin portrayed one of DC's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:08.720
more obscure anti-heroes: the bounty hunter&nbsp;
Jonah Hex. While the 2010 Jonah Hex film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:13.360
alters the character somewhat by giving him&nbsp;
the mystical power of resurrecting the dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:18.560
it does at least retain Hex's most distinctive&nbsp;
feature - his heavily scarred face.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:22.160
In the movie, Hex is disfigured after his&nbsp;
Confederate commanding officer Quentin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:27.760
Turnbull brands Hex's face with his initials.&nbsp;
Hex then removes the brand by intentionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:32.400
scarring that half of his face. Later,&nbsp;
a smart-mouthed thug asks what happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:36.560
to his face while brandishing a knife.&nbsp;
Hex guns him down, then simply quips:

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:40.222
"Cut myself shaving. What happened to yours?"

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.440
Green Lantern is a movie so thoroughly mocked that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:47.120
even its star Ryan Reynolds has&nbsp;
taken a well-deserved shot at it.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:48.656
"You're welcome, Canada."

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:53.600
Despite the film's problems, however, there is&nbsp;
one standout scene, in which Hal recites the Green&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:58.800
Lantern Oath and is propelled through a wormhole&nbsp;
to Oa, the home planet of the Green Lantern Corps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.800 --> 00:15:04.320
Hal flies over a vast sprawl of futuristic&nbsp;
buildings, past the central Power Battery, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:09.440
into an assembly of Lanterns, who reveal that he's&nbsp;
now part of something much bigger than himself.

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:16.080
The Dark Knight Rises offers one iconic scene&nbsp;
that's actually deceptively low-key for a Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:20.800
movie. In the film's final scenes, Bruce's butler&nbsp;
Alfred Pennyworth takes a holiday to Italy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:26.000
spies Bruce sitting at a nearby table with Selina&nbsp;
Kyle, having finally let go of his vigilante&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:31.280
lifestyle. Alfred then leaves, happy that Bruce&nbsp;
has found happiness outside of Gotham City.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:36.560
Unlike other Batman films, which suggest that the&nbsp;
Dark Knight is eternally locked into his crusade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:40.800
Christopher Nolan's Dark Knight&nbsp;
trilogy offers something new: closure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:46.758
Here, and nowhere else, Bruce Wayne is finally&nbsp;
able to enjoy the happy ending he deserves.

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:51.200
Zack Snyder's Man of Steel is&nbsp;
full of controversial choices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:54.480
From the movie's grim tone to&nbsp;
Superman's decision to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:59.120
General Zod at the story's climax, this&nbsp;
film continues to divide fans to this day.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:04.720
One scene, however, truly embraces the joy of&nbsp;
being Superman. Upon discovering a Kryptonian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:09.520
scout ship in the Arctic, Clark Kent dons&nbsp;
his Kryptonian suit and steps into the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:15.440
ready to learn how to fly. Gradually, Clark&nbsp;
progresses from super leaps to actual flight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.440 --> 00:16:19.120
soaring through clouds and canyons&nbsp;
before finally reaching orbit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:24.000
The look on Superman's face as he realizes&nbsp;
what he can be is genuinely heart-warming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.520
and demonstrates the true&nbsp;
potential of Superman at his best.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:34.080
Batman v Superman attracted all kinds of criticism&nbsp;
when it was released in 2016, but one thing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:38.640
movie did get right was the titular showdown&nbsp;
between the Man of Steel and the Dark Knight.

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:45.040
"Tell me… do you bleed?"

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:49.120
This sequence sees an older,&nbsp;
deadlier Bruce Wayne employ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:53.840
everything from sonic cannons to Kryptonite&nbsp;
gas, to gain the edge on his god-like opponent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:57.360
And it works, too - although the two&nbsp;
eventually stop fighting to team up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:02.000
Batman definitely gets in a good few hits&nbsp;
against Superman, and, at one point, almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:07.280
takes him down for good. It's a potent reminder of&nbsp;
just how powerful a well-prepared Batman can be.

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:12.080
While Suicide Squad received countless&nbsp;
dire reviews from the critics, many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.080 --> 00:17:16.800
them at least praised both the action and Margot&nbsp;
Robbie's performance as Harley Quinn. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:21.440
it's another character, Will Smith's Deadshot,&nbsp;
who stars in the movie's most iconic scene.

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:25.040
When his team ends up fighting an army&nbsp;
of demonic monsters, the master assassin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:29.440
Deadshot manages to convince the villains&nbsp;
to fight alongside the military to survive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:35.040
Then, just as the demons threaten to overwhelm&nbsp;
them, Deadshot steps up. He uses his uncanny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:40.160
aim and an arsenal of weaponry to take out&nbsp;
every single demon, even as they get close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:44.720
enough to tear him apart. By the end of the&nbsp;
film, Deadshot is still anything but a hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:49.120
but he has still managed to earn the respect&nbsp;
of even his hard-nosed military handler.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:54.480
Wonder Woman's best scene perfectly&nbsp;
communicates exactly who Wonder Woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:58.800
is, and where her strengths lie as a hero.&nbsp;
After accompanying Steve Trevor to Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:03.120
to help fight World War I, Diana finds&nbsp;
herself on the frontlines in Belgium.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:06.880
Here, Steve claims that moving through the&nbsp;
battlefield is more or less impossible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:10.960
as the men have been fighting for months and&nbsp;
barely gained an inch of ground in that time.

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:16.960
In response, Diana removes her cloak, climbs&nbsp;
the parapet and begins to walk across no man's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:22.400
land. A barrage of bullets and bombs try to bring&nbsp;
her down, but the Amazonian princess refuses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:28.000
give up, eventually leading the Allied forces to&nbsp;
victory. It's a stunningly-shot action sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:32.844
that really shows Wonder Woman for the&nbsp;
inspirational symbol of strength that she is.

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:38.560
The theatrical release of Justice League&nbsp;
greatly altered Zack Snyder's original vision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:43.120
As such, it's also deeply unpopular among&nbsp;
the fans - who successfully lobbied for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:46.640
the release of Zack Snyder's&nbsp;
Justice League in March 2021.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:52.560
One scene, however, did excite many fans back&nbsp;
in 2017. After being resurrected by an alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:58.640
"Mother Box," Superman emerges from his tomb in an&nbsp;
amnesiac state, and almost immediately attacks the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:04.000
newly-formed Justice League. Their combined&nbsp;
might isn't nearly enough to restrain him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:08.960
of course, and he even proves himself fast&nbsp;
enough to keep up with the Flash himself.

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:12.960
Obviously, Superman later returns as the&nbsp;
Justice League's ally - but this scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:16.800
did a pretty great job in showing why you&nbsp;
don't mess with the last son of Krypton.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:21.760
Fully embracing the fantastical&nbsp;
aspects of the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:26.640
Aquaman immerses its hero in an Atlantis&nbsp;
bursting with color and life. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:31.840
he accepts a quest to recover a powerful trident&nbsp;
that will grant him control over all sea life.

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:36.240
What this really means is made clear in the&nbsp;
movie's climactic battle scene, in which Aquaman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:42.320
shows up to an all-out war between opposing&nbsp;
undersea kingdoms. Drawing on his enhanced powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:48.480
Aquaman summons an army of sharks, whales,&nbsp;
and sea monsters that utterly overwhelms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:52.880
his opponents. It might sound like&nbsp;
a silly power on paper, but Aquaman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:58.240
showed - for the first time on screen - that&nbsp;
Arthur Curry's powers really are no joke.

00:19:59.280 --> 00:20:04.560
At the heart of Shazam! is every kid's&nbsp;
wish: to become an all-powerful superhero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:08.000
The first half of the film takes a&nbsp;
comedic approach to this premise, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:13.101
14-year-old Billy Batson gains the ability&nbsp;
to transform into a fully adult superhero.

00:20:13.101 --> 00:20:16.255
"Hey, to fighting bad guys.

00:20:21.308 --> 00:20:22.991
That tastes like actual vomit."

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:28.480
However, the film's best scene comes at the&nbsp;
climax, when Billy stumbles onto a unique solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:34.880
to stop the evil Dr. Sivana and his Seven Deadly&nbsp;
Sins: sharing his power with his foster siblings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:40.160
As the kids all shout "Shazam!," a bolt of&nbsp;
lightning transforms them into fully-grown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:45.360
superheroes, ready to take on Sivana's army. While&nbsp;
comic book fans might already be familiar with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:51.280
Shazam family, this scene will doubtless surprise&nbsp;
and delight all kinds of fans in equal measure.

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:58.960
Joker is a movie that managed to do what no other&nbsp;
DC film does: turn the Clown Prince of Crime into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:04.240
a genuinely sympathetic character. The constant&nbsp;
abuse levied against failed comedian Arthur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:08.720
Fleck comes to a head when he's invited onto a&nbsp;
late night talk show hosted by Murray Franklin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:13.840
the man he once idolized. Having previously&nbsp;
been pushed to the edge by Murray's ridicule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:18.560
Arthur - now fully inhabiting the Joker&nbsp;
persona - decides to take his revenge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:21.440
Originally planning to shoot&nbsp;
himself on live television,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:25.920
Arthur changes his mind at the last second...&nbsp;
and shoots Murray in the face instead.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:30.320
No longer an insecure sad-sack, this&nbsp;
is a Joker who has fully embraced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.320 --> 00:21:35.040
his demons - showing how the most human&nbsp;
supervillains can be the very worst of all.

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:41.040
Margot Robbie reprised her role as the Joker's&nbsp;
ex-girlfriend Harley Quinn in Birds of Prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:46.240
and the result is an off-the-wall film filled&nbsp;
with delirious fun. The full title of the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.240 --> 00:21:50.640
speaks to the film's broader aims, while&nbsp;
its best scene perfectly communicates them:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:53.680
It's all about helping Harley&nbsp;
and the Birds find themselves.

00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:58.960
After being dumped by the Joker, Harley decides&nbsp;
to reinvent herself by blowing up Ace Chemicals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:03.360
the factory where the Joker transformed her&nbsp;
into his chalk-white counterpart. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:08.240
Harley makes this decision when she's drunk and&nbsp;
feeling impulsive, it's still a fantastic moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:13.280
of rebirth that decisively separates Harley&nbsp;
from the Joker's influence. As the factory goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:18.800
up in an explosion of flaming green smoke and&nbsp;
Charlotte Lawrence's "The Joke's on You" plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:23.920
in the background, all of Gotham City learns&nbsp;
that Harley Quinn is finally her own woman.

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:28.640
Wonder Woman 1984 shows off a&nbsp;
more vulnerable side to Diana;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:32.640
one who accidentally wishes for her dead&nbsp;
lover Steve Trevor to return to her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:38.240
Her wish comes true, but as a consequence,&nbsp;
Diana's power begins to fade. This becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:42.880
a problem when she and Steve travel to Egypt&nbsp;
where the film's antagonist, Maxwell Lord,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:48.480
has taken control of an oil tycoon's personal&nbsp;
army. As Wonder Woman takes on the entire convoy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:53.120
she discovers her reduced strength, speed,&nbsp;
and stamina make the battle much harder.

00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:56.240
It's a very different side to Wonder&nbsp;
Woman, especially compared to the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:59.680
we saw storming No Man's Land in&nbsp;
the first movie. Despite it all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:03.680
she refuses to back down - and that's&nbsp;
what makes the scene so memorable.

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:10.320
One thing most people agree on about Zack&nbsp;
Snyder's vision of the DCEU is that it's all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:14.880
pretty dark. This is made abundantly&nbsp;
clear in Zack Snyder's Justice League,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:19.600
and arguably the film's most iconic&nbsp;
scene is the most despondent of all.

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:22.640
For its epilogue, the movie&nbsp;
returns to the apocalyptic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:25.760
"Knightmare" future first&nbsp;
seen in Batman v Superman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:30.640
in which Superman has turned to evil and Earth&nbsp;
has been overrun with Darkseid's Parademons.

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:34.720
Most surprising of all, Batman has entered&nbsp;
into an alliance with his archenemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:39.120
the Joker. The partnership is purely one&nbsp;
of convenience, of course, and the Joker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:42.720
still takes sick joy in tormenting&nbsp;
the Dark Knight with his failures.

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:46.480
"And I'm happy to discuss with you, in any&nbsp;
way you like, why you sent a boy wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:50.234
to do a man's job."

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:54.800
Naturally, Batman promises to one day&nbsp;
make his archenemy really pay for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.800 --> 00:23:59.360
crimes. But this is a dynamic rarely&nbsp;
depicted in DC's live-action movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:03.600
and it'd no doubt be fascinating to&nbsp;
see it further explored in the future.

